{0 Silica}

Silica is an OCaml view framework. All behaviour is in statecharts: each
component is a machine and a view. The same code runs on the server (native
OCaml) and in the browser (Melange). Hosts run the machines: the browser, the
server, and the model checker.

{1:ideas The main ideas}

- {b One machine for each component.} The states of a machine are the
  constructors of a GADT. A state owns its timers, calls and subscriptions:
  they start when the machine enters the state and stop when it leaves it.
- {b Views in JSX.} A view is a function of the snapshot, written in mlx, the
  JSX syntax for OCaml. An event handler is an event value, not code.
- {b Three kinds of data.} Local data is owned by the client. Remote data
  comes from a typed endpoint, one request for each call. Live data is owned
  by the server and sent to the client when it changes.
- {b Server render and hydration.} The server sends the first HTML with the
  answers of its calls. The browser adopts that HTML and does not call again.
- {b A typed router.} The routes are the states of a machine. One value reads
  and writes the URL of each route.
- {b A model checker.} It runs every event in every state that a component
  can reach, and checks the properties of each view. The shape of each machine
  exports to Quint.
- {b Headless components.} [silica-ui] has components with the WAI-ARIA
  patterns, after Base UI, and themes.

